The culprit is finding `crime_00022`, “Sync I/O in Hot Path.” Its evidence is:

> `existsSync` @L12 in `loadPlanSchema` (domain function)  
> `statSync` @L13 in `loadPlanSchema` (domain function)  
> `readFileSync` @L15 in `loadPlanSchema` (domain function)  
> lines: 12, 13, 15  
> swap for the async variant (`readFile`, `writeFile`, `exec`, …) and `await` it

The blocking methods are `existsSync`, `statSync`, and `readFileSync`.

The smallest migration is to use `node:fs/promises`:

```ts
import { readFile, stat } from "node:fs/promises";

async function loadPlanSchema(path: string) {
  try {
    const [fileStat, contents] = await Promise.all([
      stat(path),
      readFile(path, "utf8"),
    ]);

    // Preserve the existing validation/parsing and return behavior.
    return parsePlanSchema(contents, fileStat);
  } catch (error) {
    if (
      error instanceof Error &&
      "code" in error &&
      error.code === "ENOENT"
    ) {
      // Preserve the old existsSync(false) behavior.
      return undefined;
    }

    throw error;
  }
}
```

Then `await loadPlanSchema(...)` inside the existing request handler. This removes `existsSync` entirely—attempting the async operations and handling `ENOENT` avoids a redundant existence check and its check/use race.

Keep the exported handler’s parameters and response contract unchanged; only its internal implementation becomes asynchronous. If `loadPlanSchema` itself is exported with a synchronous return type, that exact signature cannot truthfully be preserved while eliminating blocking I/O: it must return a `Promise`, or an async internal replacement must be introduced and awaited by the already-async public handler.
